+<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->
+
 # Parameter Ontology — Layered / Contextual Namespaces
 
 This directory defines a reusable parameter ontology for the `ai-coding` plugin. The angle is **layered / contextual namespaces**: every parameter lives in a *named layer* identified by a prefix (`command.*`, `subagent.*`, `skill.*`, `shared.*`). The same root name (`task`, `parallel`, `criteria`, `model`, …) can — and is encouraged to — appear in multiple layers with different semantics. Disambiguation is done by layer prefix, **never by rename**.
diff --git a/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/command.md b/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/command.md
index 7fe0218..998996a 100644
--- a/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/command.md
+++ b/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/command.md
@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@
+<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->
+
 # `command.*` — Parameters of a Top-Level Command Invocation
 
 The `command.*` namespace describes the **orchestration plane**: every parameter the user can supply (or that defaults) when invoking a top-level slash command. A `command.*` parameter is read by the orchestrator that runs the command, NOT by the subagents the orchestrator may launch. When a value needs to flow into subagent slots, the orchestrator either broadcasts it, zips a list across slots, or rewrites it — those rewrite rules live in `cross-layer.md`.
diff --git a/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/cross-layer.md b/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/cross-layer.md
index 17883cb..d090ed4 100644
--- a/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/cross-layer.md
+++ b/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/cross-layer.md
@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@
+<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->
+
 # `cross-layer.md` — Inheritance, Overrides, Shadowing
 
 This file is the contract between layers. It exists because the same root name (`task`, `model`, `parallel`, `criteria`, …) appears in two or more `*.md` layer files with deliberately different semantics, and somebody has to define *how a value flows between them*.
diff --git a/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/shared.md b/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/shared.md
index 3b012ae..60e0d82 100644
--- a/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/shared.md
+++ b/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/shared.md
@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@
+<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->
+
 # `shared.*` — Primitive Value Types and Addressing Forms
 
 The `shared.*` namespace holds value-level building blocks that any other layer (`command.*`, `subagent.*`, `skill.*`) can compose with its domain parameters. **Shared definitions never carry orchestration semantics on their own**: they describe shapes and validation, not what a parameter *means* in a given layer. When a layer parameter says "type: `shared.int_range >= 1`", the layer file owns the meaning; this file owns the formalism.
diff --git a/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/skill.md b/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/skill.md
index eba0f21..08160e5 100644
--- a/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/skill.md
+++ b/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/skill.md
@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@
+<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->
+
 # `skill.*` — Parameters of a Skill (Activation Plane)
 
 The `skill.*` namespace describes the **activation plane**: parameters that define when and how a skill fires, what it promises, and what it contributes to whatever command or subagent activated it. Skills do NOT have an invocation parameter list the way commands do — they are auto-activated by the agent runtime when their `skill.task` description matches the situation. The parameters in this layer therefore describe a **declaration**, not a **call**.
diff --git a/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/subagent.md b/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/subagent.md
index c3bf45c..145d2e0 100644
--- a/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/subagent.md
+++ b/ai-coding/parameters/generated/layered/subagent.md
@@ -1,3 +1,5 @@
+<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->
+
 # `subagent.*` — Parameters of a Single Subagent Slot
 
 The `subagent.*` namespace describes the **execution plane**: every parameter that lives *inside one subagent slot*. A `subagent.*` parameter is read by that single subagent (typically supplied to it by the orchestrator at launch time) or reported back by it. It is NEVER the same value as the like-named `command.*` parameter, even when the orchestrator broadcasts unchanged — the semantic scope shrinks to "this slot only".
